Objective
I learnt how network ports work and how to identify the process and service associated with an open/listening port.

Concepts learned
A port is like a door through which network communication can reach a service.
TCP provides connection-oriented, reliable communication.
UDP is connectionless and generally faster but doesn't guarantee delivery.
LISTENING means a service is waiting for incoming connections.
A PID (Process ID) identifies a running process.
svchost.exe can host Windows services.
Port 135 is associated with Windows RPC services.
An open port isn't automatically a vulnerability.


Commands practiced
netstat -ano
tasklist
tasklist /svc | findstr "500"

My practical investigation
Port: 135
Protocol: TCP
State: LISTENING
PID: 500
Process: svchost.exe
Services: RpcEptMapper, RpcSs


Common ports learned
22    SSH
53    DNS
80    HTTP
443   HTTPS
3389  RDP

Security lesson
An open port does not automatically mean a system is compromised. 
A security analyst should investigate what service is behind the port, why it is exposed, who can reach it, and whether that exposure is necessary
